### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

Currently the spec says that [each form element has a planned navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#form-submission-algorithm:planned-navigation), which is set when the form is submitted and gets cancelled if it is submitted again before the navigation actually happens. However, I think it makes more sense to have one planned navigation per navigable, and that follows actual browser behaviour more closely if I understand correctly. Specifically, consider the odd difference illustrated here:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<form id=form1></form>
<form id=form2></form>
<button id=sameForm>Same form</button>
<button id=diffForm>Different form</button>
<pre id=log></pre>
<script>
navigation.addEventListener('navigate', e => { log.append('navigate\n'); e.intercept(); });

sameForm.onclick = () => {
  // This fires 1 navigate event
  form1.submit();
  form1.submit(); // cancels previous submission
};
diffForm.onclick = () => {
  // This fires 2 navigate events according to spec?
  form1.submit();
  form2.submit(); // does not cancel previous submission
};
</script>
```
In practice both WebKit and Chrome only fire 1 navigate, which aligns with one planned navigation per navigable. Gecko fires 2 in both cases because it currently does form submission synchronously but we are working on fixing that - see [bug 1998887](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1998887)
